;; cljfmt configuration.
;;
;; `:align-*-columns?` ask cljfmt to vertically align let-binding values,
;; case branches, map values, etc. across consecutive lines.
;; `:blank-lines-separate-alignment?` keeps a blank line treated as a hard
;; break so unrelated chunks don't all line up against the widest key in
;; the file.
;;
;; `:remove-multiple-non-indenting-spaces?` is left at its default (false)
;; so any hand-tuned alignment (e.g. the FFI spec table in `ducktape.ffi`)
;; is preserved as-is.
;;
;; `:extra-indents` merges into cljfmt's built-in indent rules. We use
;; `[:block 1]` for body-wrapping macros whose first arg is a header
;; (phase keyword, name, etc.) and remaining args are a body — same
;; pattern as `when`, `if`, `with-open`. This makes the body 2-indent
;; from the form's opening paren instead of aligning under the first
;; arg, which keeps deeply-nested call sites from drifting hard right.
;;
;; The same rule is also declared as `{:style/indent 1}` metadata on
;; the macro itself, which is what Emacs/CIDER and clojure-lsp read.
;; cljfmt's CLI doesn't read source metadata directly, so this config
;; is the canonical source for command-line / CI invocations.
{:align-form-columns?             true
 :align-map-columns?              true
 :blank-lines-separate-alignment? true
 ;; Actively pull whole-line `;;` comments to the surrounding code's
 ;; indent column. cljfmt's default is `false` (it only reflows comments
 ;; that fall inside a form's natural indent range); enabling this
 ;; cleans up comments that were left stranded when surrounding code
 ;; was re-indented. Top-level `;; ----` section dividers stay at
 ;; column 0 because the code they sit between is also at column 0,
 ;; so there's nothing to realign to.
 :indent-line-comments?           true
 :extra-indents                   {instr-time [[:block 1]]}}
